<ProjectContext>
# TP DECK (Techyparts Data Entry Control Keeper)
**Goal:** Automate repetitive inventory data entry between eBay, ShipStation, and RazorERP without creating a new browser for each run.
**Target Environment:** Native Windows multimonitor desktop utility.
**Theme Identity:** Techyparts enterprise hardware dark theme.
- Background: Deep Server Charcoal (`#0F172A` or `#1E293B`)
- Primary Accent: Enterprise Electric Blue (`#0EA5E9`)
- Text: Crisp White (`#F8FAFC`)
- Cancel/Stop: Alert Red (`#7F1D1D` background, `#FCA5A5` text)
- Update warning: pulse the action buttons between yellow (`#FBBF24`) and grey (`#64748B`)
</ProjectContext>

<TechStack>
- **Language:** Python 3.11+
- **GUI Framework:** PySide6 (Native Windows integrations, Qt.WindowStaysOnTopHint)
- **Browser Automation:** Playwright (async API) via Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP)
- **Global Hotkey:** `keyboard` library (Strictly limited to the `Pause` key)
- **Concurrency:** `asyncio` for managing cancelable background automation tasks, bridged to Qt with `qasync`
</TechStack>

<ArchitectureRules>
# 1. Packaging (STRICT)
- DO NOT use PyInstaller `--onefile` or broadly hook the Windows keyboard API.
- Global hotkey usage is strictly limited to registering the `Pause` (Pause/Break) key for the emergency abort function.
- Releases are an embeddable CPython zip plus source, launched by `TP-DECK.bat`. Write code assuming a standard `python main.py` execution environment.
- Do not hand-edit `__version__`. The version is the latest `v*` git tag. See GitHub releases below.

# 2. Window & UI Constraints
- **Floating Tool Window:** The main UI is a compact, floating dashboard. Use `Qt.WindowType.WindowStaysOnTopHint | Qt.WindowType.Tool`.
- **Position Persistence:** Override `closeEvent` and `moveEvent` to save the exact `(X, Y)` screen coordinates to `settings.json`. On initialization, load these coordinates so the app remembers its placement on auxiliary monitors.
- **Debouncing:** When automation starts, disable the running action button and change its text to "⏳ Processing...". Re-enable inside a `finally` block.

# 3. Automation Engine & CDP Connection
- **NO NEW BROWSERS PER RUN:** DO NOT use `playwright.chromium.launch()`. Attach with `playwright.chromium.connect_over_cdp()` to the Chromium instance TP DECK already started.
- **Operating Modes:** Support "Single" (Port 9222) and "Dual" browser connections (eBay Port 9222, ERP Port 9223) determined by `settings.json`.
- **Tab Disambiguation:** Match the configured URL pattern across CDP contexts. Use `document.hasFocus()` only when more than one tab matches, so a unique URL does not have to be the focused tab.

# 4. Emergency Kill-Switch
- All Playwright operations must run inside an `asyncio.Task`.
- Provide a red UI button `[🛑 Emergency Stop]` AND listen for the `Pause` key globally. Both triggers must call `task.cancel()` on the running asyncio task immediately.

# 5. Configuration & State Management
- Ports, mode, timeouts, hotkey, and window geometry live in `tp_deck/settings.json`. Do not hardcode them.
- DOM CSS selectors and Razor page addresses live in `tp_deck/locators.json`, not in Settings.
- Maintain a rolling log at `tp_deck/tpdeck.log`.
</ArchitectureRules>

<ProjectStructure>
/tp_deck/
  ├── main.py                 # Qt + asyncio bridge, hotkey, jobs, update check
  ├── dashboard.py            # Floating UI, theme, position persistence
  ├── settings_dialog.py      # Ports, mode, timeouts
  ├── settings.json           # Runtime config (not selectors)
  ├── locators.json           # CSS selectors and Razor URLs
  ├── automation_engine.py    # CDP connect, eBay scrape, ERP location lookup
  ├── shipstation.py          # Store-order scrape
  ├── sales_order.py          # Open a Razor sales order for batch serial
  ├── chrome_launcher.py      # Start the portable Chromium debug browser
  ├── updater.py              # GitHub release check and in-place upgrade
  └── tpdeck.log              # Rolling log
</ProjectStructure>

<GitHubReleases>
# Commit & Push means release
When the user asks to **Commit & Push** (or any clear Commit & Push phrasing), treat that as a full release, not a code-only push. Do all of the following in order, without asking again for the tag or version unless they named a specific version:

1. Read the latest `v*` tag (`git describe --tags --abbrev=0 --match "v*"` or `git tag --sort=-v:refname`).
2. Increment the patch number by default (`v0.3.1` → `v0.3.2`). Use a minor or major bump only when the user asks for one, or when they give an exact tag.
3. Commit the current changes on the working branch. Do not edit `tp_deck/__init__.py`; the tag is the version.
4. Push the branch to `origin` (`squishey123/TP-DECK`).
5. Create the new tag on that commit and push the tag:

```
git tag v0.3.2
git push origin v0.3.2
```

6. `.github/workflows/release.yml` runs on the tag. `scripts/build_release.ps1` reads the tag, builds `dist/TP-DECK-<version>-windows-x64.zip`, and stamps that version into the packaged `tp_deck/__init__.py`.
7. The workflow publishes a GitHub Release named `TP DECK <version>` and attaches that zip. The asset name must stay `TP-DECK-<version>-windows-x64.zip`; the updater only accepts that pattern.
8. **Release notes come last.** Wait until the Release workflow succeeds and the zip is on the release. Do not edit the release body while Actions is still running. The create-release step overwrites the body when it finishes, so notes written earlier are lost. After the workflow is green, update the notes with `gh release edit <tag> --notes "..."` (or the Notes editor). Summarize what changed in this version.
9. A release install checks `update_repo` (`squishey123/TP-DECK`) against the latest GitHub release the next time it opens. A newer tag downloads and replaces the app. `settings.json`, `sku_overrides.json`, `sku_location_cache.json`, `uline_cache.json`, `uline_draft.json`, and the browser profile are kept. `Uline Exports/` sits outside the package, so an update leaves it in place.

A request that says only **commit** (no push) stays a local commit. A request that says push without Commit & Push wording is a normal branch push and does not create a release tag.
</GitHubReleases>
